  "explanation": "In modern C++, move semantics enable efficient transfer of resources from temporary objects (rvalues) to new objects, reducing unnecessary copying and improving performance. Rvalue references (denoted by &&) are a key feature that allow functions and constructors to distinguish between lvalues and rvalues, facilitating move semantics. This is especially important in resource-managing classes, such as containers and smart pointers, to optimize resource transfer without expensive copying.\n\nMove semantics in C++ enable efficient transfer of resources from temporary objects (rvalues) to new objects, reducing unnecessary copying and improving performance. This is achieved through rvalue references (&&) and move constructors or move assignment operators, which 'move' resources instead of copying them. Modern C++ leverages move semantics to optimize resource management, especially in container classes and performance-critical applications.",
